Third-party notices for `rnpm` (working title — R757)
=====================================================

pnpm / pacquet
--------------

W318 §10.1 records an operator decision, adopted 2026-08-15 and binding on
every ticket under R773: this crate is written having *read* pnpm's Rust port
of its resolver (the `pnpm/crates/*` members, formerly `pnpm/pacquet`), so we
carry pnpm's MIT notice rather than claim clean-room. Where a specific routine
is ported rather than reimplemented, the call site says so and names the
upstream file.

Which parts of this crate are ports, and which are not, is stated per section
below — see the orogene section for `src/resolve.rs`'s `place`, and the pnpm
section for `src/peers.rs`. The R773-F1 layer (registry client, packument
model, on-disk cache) and the R773-F2 layer (`src/spec.rs`'s parser) are
**not** ports of pnpm code; they were written against npm's published registry
protocol and document formats. This pnpm notice is carried because the peer
post-pass in `src/peers.rs` follows `resolve_peers.rs`'s algorithm, and because
MIT requires the notice to travel with any substantial copied portion.

**`src/peers.rs` IS a port of the algorithm** (R773-F4), from
`pnpm/crates/resolving-deps-resolver/src/resolve_peers.rs` and its
`resolve_peers/{walker,cache}.rs` submodules (MIT). Taken from it: the
depth-first walk that propagates a parent-refs map down the chain; the
`peersCache`, keyed on package identity plus the resolved peer context, so that
a revisit in a compatible context short-circuits; the `purePkgs` fast path,
where a package with no peers anywhere in its subtree is skipped without
recursing, populated bottom-up; and the cycle break by an `in_progress` set over
a post-order traversal. The module header repeats this at the call site.

Not taken, because they belong to pnpm's *model* rather than to the algorithm:
`depPath` construction and its peer suffix, per-importer and catalog handling,
`link:` remapping, and the `dedupe_peer_dependents` / `hoist_peers` passes. Our
layout is npm/bun-style hoisting with nesting (W318 §6's adopted layout call),
so an instance here is a second *node in the tree* rather than a second
`depPath` key — which is why `Walker::instantiate` and `deep_copy` have no
upstream counterpart to name.

